seeking alternative markets and relying on reserves. This case highlights the impact of economic
power in weakening an aggressor’s economy, although it also shows that sanctions may not
immediately end a conflict.
Case Study 3: Russia’s Energy Leverage Over Europe (2022)
Amid the war in Ukraine, Russia used its position as a major supplier of natural gas to Europe as
an economic weapon, cutting off or reducing gas supplies to countries like Germany, Poland, and
others that supported Ukraine. This led to a severe energy crisis in Europe, causing a spike in
energy prices and forcing European governments to seek alternative energy sources.
The use of energy leverage put significant pressure on European economies, but it also
accelerated Europe’s transition to renewable energy and efforts to diversify energy supplies. This
case illustrates the power of energy resources in economic coercion but also the risks involved,
as Europe has moved to reduce its dependence on Russian energy.
Structural Power:
Structural power refers to the ability of a state or organisation to shape the frameworks and
institutions that govern global interactions and decision-making. By establishing the rules of the
game—whether through control over international institutions, trade systems, or legal
frameworks—states can indirectly influence the behavior of others in ways that benefit their
interests.
Case Study 1: The U.S. Dollar as the Global Reserve Currency
The U.S. dollar’s status as the global reserve currency is a key example of structural power.
Since the end of World War II, the dollar has been the dominant currency for international trade
and financial transactions, particularly in energy markets like oil. Most countries hold their foreign
exchange reserves in dollars, and many international debts are in the currency. This gives the
U.S. significant power over global finance.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 6
The dollar’s dominance allows the U.S. to impose effective financial sanctions on countries like
Iran, Russia, and North Korea by limiting their access to global financial systems. Countries
relying on the dollar for international trade and reserve holdings face significant economic
challenges if cut off from the U.S. financial system. This structural power gives the U.S. outsized
influence over global economic decisions.
Case Study 2: The United Nations Security Council (UNSC) Veto Power
The UNSC’s five permanent members (P5)—the U.S., UK, Russia, China, and France—each
hold veto power over any substantive resolution, allowing them to block decisions they disagree
with. This structural power ensures these states retain significant influence over global security
and peacekeeping efforts.
A recent example occurred in 2022 when Russia used its veto to block a UNSC resolution
condemning its invasion of Ukraine. Despite broad international condemnation of the invasion,
Russia’s veto ensured that no binding action could be taken by the Council to address the
conflict. The veto power also allows P5 members to advance their national interests while
preventing collective action on issues like conflicts in Syria and Yemen.
Case Study 3: China’s Belt and Road Initiative and the Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank
China’s Belt and Road Initiative (BRI), launched in 2013, has been a critical tool for expanding its
structural power by reshaping global trade routes and building economic dependencies. Through
large infrastructure projects in over 140 countries, China has created new trade links and
economic alliances, particularly in developing regions. The Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank
(AIIB), founded in 2016, serves as a financial arm of the BRI, offering loans for infrastructure
projects that follow Chinese standards and priorities.
For instance, China has financed ports, railways, and highways across Africa, Asia, and Europe,
like the China-Pakistan Economic Corridor (CPEC), a major BRI project. Many countries that
participate in the BRI become economically dependent on China due to the significant loans they
receive, and in some cases, struggle with debt repayment. For example, Sri Lanka leased its
strategic Hambantota Port to a Chinese company after failing to repay BRI loans. The AIIB’s role
in financing these projects further amplifies China’s ability to influence global economic
structures, giving it significant leverage in participating countries’ future trade and economic
decisions.
Key Recent Example - Trump’s Tariffs and Military Threats
● Since returning to the Presidency in 2025, Trump has shifted U.S. foreign policy to be far
more focused on exerting the U.S.’s hard power (including its economic and military
strength) to achieve its geopolitical goals, rather than combining this far more with soft
power as Biden did.
○ In April 2025, he introduced a 145% tariff on goods imported from China, in a clear
attempt to use America’s economic strength to harm the Chinese economy and its
status as a manufacturing superpower.
○ In April 2025, the Trump administration signed an agreement to deploy U.S. troops
along the Panama Canal to assert U.S. power and tackle growing Chinese influence
in the region.
○ Since coming into power for the second time, Trump has repeatedly threatened to
use military force to seize Greenland, as he sees it as key to U.S. security and to
geopolitical competition in the Arctic.
● Together, this represents a clear example of the use of hard power by a state and how hard
power can be seen as more significant and effective than soft power.
